# SYNOPSIS

```c
vx_status vx_trace_configure(vx_handle resource, uint32_t op, void *data,
                             uint64_t len);
```

# DESCRIPTION

The kernel's trace (ADR-0049, M7 step 7a1): one ring per CPU of fixed 32-byte records, `vx_trace_record` (`time` on the cycle counter, `kind`, `cpu`, `tid` as task id << 12 | thread id in the task, and two words), written by the CPU itself with interrupts off.
<resource> is a Resource handle with `VX_RIGHT_TRACE`, which svcd gives procfs(4) alone (svc(6)'s `trace`), or the root's MANAGE.

`VX_TRACE_START`, with <data> a `vx_trace_start`: the categories (`VX_TC_SCHED`, `VX_TC_IPC`, `VX_TC_IRQ`, `VX_TC_VM`, `VX_TC_FUTEX`, `VX_TC_SYSCALL`, `VX_TC_MARK`), the ring size per CPU (a multiple of 4096; 0 for 1 MiB) and whether a full ring drops what follows (oneshot, counting it) or overwrites the oldest (circular). The rings start empty.
`VX_TRACE_STOP` stops writing and returns once no CPU is mid-record. `VX_TRACE_REWIND` empties the rings.
`VX_TRACE_RINGS` puts at <data> (a `vx_handle`) a read-only handle to the rings' VMO: for each CPU at `i * stride`, a header page (`vx_trace_ring`: `head`, the drops and the times of the first and last, the counter's frequency) and then its records.
`VX_TRACE_MARK` writes <data>, up to 16 bytes, as one `VX_TK_MARK` record.

What each kind's words mean is in `abi/vx/abi.h` and `/proc/trace/.schema`.

